Z||000000||danny boy||04-23-2001||11:18 PM|||| Tomorrow we are going to get the new engine for Formula's car. As it sits right now, Its carbuerated. He would like to keep it that way to use the intake thats on it(lotsa port/polish work on heads and intake. I agree that it would be a good idea so the cam thats in it can be used.Way too big for stock fuel injection, but the guy had an edelbrock 390CFM 4 barrel on it. I have no idea about how to get this right with the computer. Doesn't the VSS run through the computer and my biggest thing, what about ignition? Will we have to use an old non computer dist? what will that affect? Gauges? <BR>The Blazer had an MSD setup but I'm not sure how everything worked. It is a 60*V6.<p>[This message has been edited by danny boy (edited 04-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||MontanaFiero||04-24-2001||02:41 AM||||WHY?!?!  I would go with aftermarket fuel injection before I would ever mount a toilet on top of my motor. I just don't like that flushing sound as I watch my gas needle move another inch.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Taijiguy||04-24-2001||08:25 AM||||Originally posted by MontanaFiero:<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>WHY?!?! I would go with aftermarket fuel injection before I would ever mount a toilet on top of my motor. I just don't like that flushing sound as I watch my gas needle move another inch.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>The words of a guy looking to start  a war. Probably just young and dumb...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>I have a Holley 390 on an Edelbrock intake on mine, and I love it. The only downside is there are only a couple of inches from the top of the carb to the decklid, so you have to be creative about the air filtering method. I used a distributor from an early 80's Blazer 2.8 which provides mechanical advance. The VSS is not connected to the ECM, mine is gone completely. You will need to get creative with the electric fuel pump for power, and delivery. I tapped into one of the power wires left unused after the ecm was removed. I wired it so it operates as normal, but without the ECM it won't come on for the initial pressure buildup when the key is turned on. This is OK since the bowls of course hold fuel. The problem with that is you have to devise a method of getting the fuel into the carb if it runs dry. Like intitial startup or if you run out of gas. I installed a hidden pushbutton switch that lets me override the pump power circuit through the oil sender so I can fill the fuel bowls. You'll also need to make or buy a bypass fuel regulator, since the needle valves in the carb won't stand up to the pressure of the fuel pump, and some will need to be bled off. I used a "tee" fitting and restricted the return line to give sufficient pressure to feed the carb. It sounds like alot, but it's really pretty much common sense. If you need any help, email me, I'll be glad to lend a hand, or any advice I can. It's a fun mod. I love the way the exhaust sounds with the 4bbl on there....nothing to make blood rush like the sound of the secondaries kicking in. (beats the ticking sound of squirting injectors as far as I'm concerned, but to each his own) It's true mileage isn't quite as good, but with the cam I had, the fuel injection system was lost most of the time anyway, the MAP sensor never knew what the hell it was doing since vacuum was all over the place at idle. So even with the fuel injection my mileage sucked anyway. I just figure it's the cost of going fast.<BR>~Taiji<p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 04-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mach10||04-24-2001||06:46 PM||||I've always liked carbs. What kind of improvements, if any would I be looking at on the stock 2.8, if the heads are port/polished, and roller-tip rockers w/ hot cam?<BR>Also, what kind of coinage am I looking at, for just the intake setup? I can fix the rest, easy.||||reg||5
Z||000004||rogergarrison||04-24-2001||07:25 PM||||cool. i want 3 dual port webbers. let us know how the carb works out.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Taijiguy||04-24-2001||07:39 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison<BR><B>cool. i want 3 dual port webbers. let us know how the carb works out</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Before finally settling on the Holley 390, I played around with a set of 3 270 cfm Holley/Webber carbs. These are two stage, carbs, they're a 2 bbl carb that acts like a 4 bbl, with a primary and a secondary. I bought the carbs, and had the whole thing planned out, but couldn't get access to the machine shop to fabricate the manifold...so went on and put on the 390. I love the setup even if it was second choice.<P>Costwise, the whole conversion ran maybe 400 bucks. The manifold was 2 pieces, at about 100 per, the carb I snagged on ebay for another 100, and then miscellaneous items, like the distributor, 40 bucks at a boneyard, and other stuff. Not bad for a completely custom setup. I'm still working on the air filter setup. I'm considering a shaker scoop. I have the rough casting done, but am thinking I might redesign it.<p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 04-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||danny boy||04-24-2001||11:34 PM|||| Cool. I was thinking maybe a 4cyl fuel pump and a regulator would work. This engine is bad. Lifted front tires(33's) off the ground in the Blazer. The guy origionally ran a 390. Can't beat that carb sound. I was thinking 450 Holley(I'm kinda partial to Holley) but that may be too big.  <BR>  Thanks for the help,I will be sure to email if we run into problems.<BR> Formula drove the Stealth tonight.He,he,he.||||reg||1
